<Project>

  <!--
    Native AOT / trimming friendliness is a hard requirement for the codec, gated off for test
    and benchmark projects here rather than in Directory.Build.props.

    MSBuild's implicit SDK import order is: Directory.Build.props, then the project file's own
    body, then Directory.Build.targets. $(IsTestProject) is set inside each project file's own
    body (e.g. tests/DatabentoDotNet.Dbn.Tests.csproj sets it to `true`), so a Condition that
    reads it only sees the real value once evaluation has moved past that project file — i.e.
    in a .targets file, not a .props file. Putting this PropertyGroup in Directory.Build.props
    (as it originally was) evaluates the Condition before IsTestProject is ever set, so it reads
    as empty, '' != 'true' is always true, and the analyzers run on every project including
    tests — the opposite of the intent. $(IsBenchmarkProject) is set the same way and read here
    for the same reason: BenchmarkDotNet generates a project per run and drives it by reflection,
    which the AOT analyzer is right to object to and which no consumer of this package ever runs.

    $(IsProbeProject) is the third exclusion, and it is not the same as the second.
    DatabentoDotNet.AotProbe (#64) *wants* the trim and AOT analyzers — it sets PublishAot, which
    turns both on for itself — so excluding it here costs nothing on that axis. What it must be
    excluded from is the public API lock below: the probe ships no package, and it compiles the Live
    test project's mock gateway by <Compile Link>, so RS0016 would demand a PublicAPI baseline for a
    few hundred members of a test double that has no public surface to lock.

    $(IsSampleProject) is the fourth, and it is a plain one (#66). The four projects under samples/
    are consumer-position code: they call the public API and ship nothing. The lock has nothing to
    lock — each is a single file of top-level statements, so its only type is the compiler's own
    internal Program — and RS0016 still needs both PublicAPI text files to exist before it will say
    so, which is eight empty files bought for no signal. Excluding them from $(ShippingProject) also
    takes the AOT and trim analyzers off them, which is no loss: what those would check on a sample
    is that the four packages survive being called from an analysed consumer, and
    tools/DatabentoDotNet.AotProbe answers that question by *publishing* a native binary rather than
    by asserting it in a project that is never published at all.

    Four exclusions rather than an opt-in list is still the right way round. A new *shipping*
    project that forgets to opt in would silently have no API lock and no AOT analysis, and nothing
    would say so; a new non-shipping project that forgets to opt out fails loudly on its first
    build, which is a mistake that corrects itself.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ShippingProject Condition="'$(IsTestProject)' != 'true' AND '$(IsBenchmarkProject)' != 'true' AND '$(IsProbeProject)' != 'true' AND '$(IsSampleProject)' != 'true'">true</ShippingProject>
    <IsAotCompatible Condition="'$(ShippingProject)' == 'true'">true</IsAotCompatible>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer Condition="'$(ShippingProject)' == 'true'">true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableAotAnalyzer Condition="'$(ShippingProject)' == 'true'">true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The public API lock (#63), wired here for the same reason the AOT analyzers above are: it applies
    to shipping projects only, and $(ShippingProject) is not readable until evaluation has moved past
    each project file's own body.

    Declared once here rather than four times in the project files. The alternative — a
    PackageReference and two AdditionalFiles in each of the four .csproj — is four places for a new
    shipping project to forget one, and forgetting it fails open: the project simply has no lock, and
    nothing says so.

    The two text files are per-project and live beside the project file. Normally Shipped is the
    surface of a released version and Unshipped is what has accumulated since, moved across at each
    release. Both must exist even when empty, or RS0016 cannot tell "not yet declared" from "no
    file" — which is what the Error target below is for, and it is not decorative: the package
    itself adds them, but only `Condition="Exists(...)"`, so a shipping project that has neither
    file gets no diagnostic from anywhere and its surface is silently unlocked.

    This ItemGroup used to include both files explicitly as well, and that was a duplicate rather
    than a belt-and-braces (#67). The package's buildTransitive targets already do it, so every
    shipping project carried two AdditionalFiles items pointing at the same path. csc tolerates
    that; Roslyn's MSBuildWorkspace does not, and DocFX — which loads the projects through it to
    extract the API — reported eight "Duplicate source file" warnings, two per package. Dropping
    our copy was checked against the thing it exists for rather than assumed safe: with the lines
    gone, deleting one entry from PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt still fails the build with RS0016 naming
    the symbol.

    Here the entire baseline sits in Unshipped and Shipped is empty, which is the honest placement:
    nothing has been published to NuGet, so nothing has shipped. #68 moves it across when 1.0.0
    goes out, and that move is the release's own reviewable diff.

    That placement was checked rather than assumed, because it would have been the wrong one if
    RS0017 only policed the Shipped file — removals would then have gone unreported, which is half
    of what this lock is for. It does police both: changing an Unshipped entry's value produces
    RS0016 for the real member and RS0017 for the stale one, and both are errors here. Verified by
    doing it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(ShippingProject)' == 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" PrivateAssets="all" IncludeAssets="runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The "both files must exist" half of the lock, stated rather than implied (#67).

    Before #67 this was an accident of the explicit AdditionalFiles that issue removed: a missing
    file produced CS2001, "Source file could not be found", which stops the build without
    explaining which rule was being enforced or what to do. Now that the package's own conditional include is the only
    one, a shipping project with no baseline files would simply have no lock, so the condition is
    checked here on purpose and says what it wants.
  -->
  <Target Name="EnsurePublicApiBaselineExists" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile" Condition="'$(ShippingProject)' == 'true'">
    <Error Condition="!Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/PublicAPI.Shipped.txt') OR !Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt')"
           Text="$(MSBuildProjectName) is a shipping project, so its public API is locked by RS0016 — but that needs both PublicAPI.Shipped.txt and PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt next to the project file, and at least one is missing. Create them (empty is a valid starting point; the baseline goes in Unshipped until #68 ships it), or mark the project IsTestProject/IsBenchmarkProject/IsProbeProject/IsSampleProject if it does not ship." />
  </Target>

</Project>
